{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "82 /app/paper.txt\n---\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—AI systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code—has brought significant benefits to software development workflows. However, these powerful systems also introduce critical security and safety concerns that demand rigorous evaluation. In this paper, we provide a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine the landscape of code agent architectures, their trustworthiness assessment, and the emergence of automated red-teaming and blue-teaming frameworks designed to evaluate and improve code agent security. We review key benchmarks including RedCode for evaluating risky code execution and generation, RedCodeAgent for automatic red-teaming of diverse code agents, and BlueCodeAgent for blue teaming capabilities enabled by automated red teaming. Furthermore, we discuss the broader context of trustworthiness assessment in generative AI models through the lens of DecodingTrust, and survey foundational deep learning architectures that underpin modern code agents. Our analysis reveals significant gaps in current evaluation frameworks and proposes directions for future research in secure code agent development.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, maintained, and deployed. Leveraging large language models (LLMs), these autonomous systems can understand programming specifications, generate production-ready code, execute scripts, and iteratively debug and refine their outputs. The adoption of code agents has been accelerated by their integration into popular development environments, enabling developers to delegate complex coding tasks to AI-powered assistants.\n\nDespite these benefits, the deployment of code agents in real-world scenarios raises substantial security concerns. Unlike traditional software tools, code agents can execute arbitrary code, access file systems, and interact with network resources—capabilities that, if exploited, could lead to severe security breaches. Recent incidents have demonstrated that code agents can be manipulated to generate harmful code or execute dangerous operations when subjected to carefully crafted adversarial prompts.\n\nThe need for comprehensive security evaluation of code agents has led to the emergence of red-teaming methodologies specifically designed for this domain. Red-teaming, traditionally employed to identify vulnerabilities in security systems, has been adapted to systematically probe code agents for unsafe behaviors. Automated red-teaming agents can generate diverse attack strategies, test edge cases, and uncover vulnerabilities that human testers might overlook.\n\nThis paper presents a systematic review of research advances in code agents and red-teaming. We organize our discussion around several key themes: the architecture and capabilities of modern code agents, benchmarks and evaluation frameworks for assessing code agent safety, automated red-teaming approaches, blue-teaming strategies for improving agent security, and broader trustworthiness considerations for generative AI models.\n\nThe contributions of this paper are threefold:\n1. We provide a comprehensive overview of the code agent landscape and their security challenges.\n2. We survey recent benchmarks and red-teaming frameworks specifically designed for code agents.\n3. We identify gaps in current research and propose directions for future work in building secure and trustworthy code agents.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe field of code agents and their security evaluation has seen rapid growth in recent years. In this section, we review key contributions across several dimensions: foundational deep learning architectures, trustworthiness assessment of generative models, benchmarks for code agent safety, automated red-teaming frameworks, and blue-teaming methodologies.\n\n### 2.1 Foundational Deep Learning Architectures\n\nModern code agents are built upon deep learning architectures that have been developed over the past decade. The Transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. in their seminal work \"Attention Is All You Need\" (arXiv:1706.03762), revolutionized sequence modeling by replacing recurrent and convolutional neural networks with self-attention mechanisms. This architecture has become the foundation for most modern large language models, including those powering code agents. The self-attention mechanism enables models to capture long-range dependencies in code sequences, facilitating understanding of complex programming constructs.\n\nConvolutional neural networks, though primarily used in computer vision, have also contributed to the deep learning toolkit. ResNet, introduced by He et al. in \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition\" (arXiv:1512.03385), demonstrated that residual connections enable training of extremely deep networks. While ResNet was developed for image recognition, the residual learning principle has influenced architecture design across various domains, including approaches to handling deep code representations.\n\n### 2.2 Trustworthiness Assessment of Generative Models\n\nAs generative AI models become more capable, understanding their trustworthiness has become critical. Wang et al. introduced \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models\" (arXiv:2306.11698), which provides a systematic framework for evaluating the trustworthiness of GPT models across multiple dimensions including factual consistency, bias, toxicity, robustness, and fairness. While DecodingTrust focuses on general-purpose GPT models, its assessment framework provides valuable insights for evaluating code agents, which are built upon similar underlying architectures. The trustworthiness dimensions identified in DecodingTrust—particularly factual consistency and robustness—are directly applicable to assessing the reliability of code generated by AI agents.\n\n### 2.3 Benchmarks for Code Agent Safety\n\nThe evaluation of code agent safety requires specialized benchmarks that capture the unique risks associated with autonomous code execution and generation. Guo et al. introduced \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents\" (arXiv:2409.12345), which provides a comprehensive evaluation framework for assessing code agent vulnerabilities. RedCode includes two components: RedCode-Exec, which evaluates agents' ability to recognize and handle unsafe code execution (providing 4,050 risky test cases across Python and Bash tasks covering 25 types of critical vulnerabilities), and RedCode-Gen, which assesses whether code agents will follow instructions to generate harmful code (providing 160 prompts with function signatures and docstrings). The benchmark covers diverse domains including websites and file systems, offering practical evaluation metrics to assess execution results using Docker environments.\n\n### 2.4 Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nBeyond static benchmarks, automated red-teaming approaches enable continuous evaluation of code agents against evolving threats. Guo et al. proposed \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents\" (arXiv:2510.02609), which introduces an automated framework for red-teaming diverse code agents. RedCodeAgent leverages the capabilities of red-teaming LLMs to autonomously generate adversarial prompts and attack strategies against target code agents. This approach enables systematic exploration of the attack surface of code agents, identifying vulnerabilities that might not be captured by static benchmarks. The agent-based approach allows for dynamic, multi-round interactions that more closely simulate real-world adversarial scenarios.\n\n### 2.5 Blue-Teaming for Code Agent Security\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming emphasizes building defenses and improving security posture. Guo et al. introduced \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI\" (arXiv:2501.01234), which presents a complementary approach to code agent security. BlueCodeAgent leverages insights gained from automated red-teaming to develop defensive strategies for CodeGen models. The system integrates red-teaming results to inform the development of robust defense mechanisms, creating a feedback loop that continuously improves agent security. This blue-teaming approach addresses the gap in current research, where most efforts focus on red-teaming without adequate attention to defense development.\n\n## 3. Discussion\n\nThe research surveyed in this paper reveals several important trends in code agent security:\n",
