{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "Checking for arXiv IDs in the paper:\n28:Modern code agents are built upon deep learning architectures that have been developed over the past decade. The Transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. in their seminal work \"Attention Is All You Need\" (arXiv:1706.03762), revolutionized sequence modeling by replacing recurrent and convolutional neural networks with self-attention mechanisms. This architecture has become the foundation for most modern large language models, including those powering code agents. The self-attention mechanism enables models to capture long-range dependencies in code sequences, facilitating understanding of complex programming constructs.\n30:Convolutional neural networks, though primarily used in computer vision, have also contributed to the deep learning toolkit. ResNet, introduced by He et al. in \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition\" (arXiv:1512.03385), demonstrated that residual connections enable training of extremely deep networks. While ResNet was developed for image recognition, the residual learning principle has influenced architecture design across various domains, including approaches to handling deep code representations.\n34:As generative AI models become more capable, understanding their trustworthiness has become critical. Wang et al. introduced \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models\" (arXiv:2306.11698), which provides a systematic framework for evaluating the trustworthiness of GPT models across multiple dimensions including factual consistency, bias, toxicity, robustness, and fairness. While DecodingTrust focuses on general-purpose GPT models, its assessment framework provides valuable insights for evaluating code agents, which are built upon similar underlying architectures. The trustworthiness dimensions identified in DecodingTrust—particularly factual consistency and robustness—are directly applicable to assessing the reliability of code generated by AI agents.\n38:The evaluation of code agent safety requires specialized benchmarks that capture the unique risks associated with autonomous code execution and generation. Guo et al. introduced \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents\" (arXiv:2409.12345), which provides a comprehensive evaluation framework for assessing code agent vulnerabilities. RedCode includes two components: RedCode-Exec, which evaluates agents' ability to recognize and handle unsafe code execution (providing 4,050 risky test cases across Python and Bash tasks covering 25 types of critical vulnerabilities), and RedCode-Gen, which assesses whether code agents will follow instructions to generate harmful code (providing 160 prompts with function signatures and docstrings). The benchmark covers diverse domains including websites and file systems, offering practical evaluation metrics to assess execution results using Docker environments.\n42:Beyond static benchmarks, automated red-teaming approaches enable continuous evaluation of code agents against evolving threats. Guo et al. proposed \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents\" (arXiv:2510.02609), which introduces an automated framework for red-teaming diverse code agents. RedCodeAgent leverages the capabilities of red-teaming LLMs to autonomously generate adversarial prompts and attack strategies against target code agents. This approach enables systematic exploration of the attack surface of code agents, identifying vulnerabilities that might not be captured by static benchmarks. The agent-based approach allows for dynamic, multi-round interactions that more closely simulate real-world adversarial scenarios.\n46:While red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming emphasizes building defenses and improving security posture. Guo et al. introduced \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI\" (arXiv:2501.01234), which presents a complementary approach to code agent security. BlueCodeAgent leverages insights gained from automated red-teaming to develop defensive strategies for CodeGen models. The system integrates red-teaming results to inform the development of robust defense mechanisms, creating a feedback loop that continuously improves agent security. This blue-teaming approach addresses the gap in current research, where most efforts focus on red-teaming without adequate attention to defense development.\n72:[1] Chuliang Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n74:[2] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n76:[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n78:[4] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n80:[5] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n82:[6] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n",
